From bc3bf624aa67b48329d9512da9a03141c9b88db9 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: godpow <17231911+godpow@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sat, 31 Oct 2026 09:10:23 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 1/3] macro: compile Turing macros to ARM64

Switch 2 titles on Android interpreted every MME macro call: the
compiled macro code (macro_turing_jit.h) is x86-64 only, so ARM64 ran
the macro memo and the interpreter on the GPU thread for the ~1M calls
a second of a 3D scene.

macro_turing_jit_arm64.h ports TuringMacroJit to ARM64 with oaknut, op
by op and with the same interface and semantics: State layout,
callbacks, errors, the END delay slot, loops, carry, predication, lane
1's operands read before lane 0 writes, the instruction limit counted
on indirect jumps. The code goes into its own pages, written and then
made read-only and executable with the instruction cache invalidated
(macro_turing_jit_arm64.cpp). Conditional jumps to the shared exits go
through stubs placed every 256 KiB, as B.cond reaches only 1 MiB (all
4096 instructions take ~660 KiB). 3D and compute macros use it like
the x86-64 code; EDEN_NX2_MME_JIT=0 still goes back to the interpreter.

Nobody can debug it on a phone, so it checks itself: after a compile,
the first 64 calls of each entry also run the interpreter on a copy of
their inputs. Only the compiled code calls out; the interpreter replays
the register reads, and the FIFO and data RAM words the emits brought
in, and every emit, read, the result, FIFO use and the data RAM are
compared. A difference is logged once (with the call's parameters and
code) and that program goes back to the memo and the interpreter.
Calls the compiled code runs differently on purpose (never-written
registers read as 0, the jump-counted limit, a failing instruction
reporting another lane's error) are not compared.

Checked offline: the ARM64 code, run in Unicorn, matched the x86-64
code run natively on 24,720 calls of 1500 random programs, including
4096-instruction ones (final State, emits, reads, FIFO, data RAM,
callee-saved registers, stack), with caller-saved registers and the
upper bits of callback results trashed at every call; six injected
codegen bugs were each caught. The self-check, run on the host against
the x86-64 code, matched on 9,095 checked calls and caught injected
data RAM, FIFO, emit and read differences.

Assisted-by: Claude Opus 5.5 (implementation and debugging)
(cherry picked from commit 9c110d38936a666c9c835e7183b5f9b8f1ad4323)
